FILE: IndyDevDan/book/_extracts/ma-2-lead-agents/extract.md
# Extract — Multi-Team Agentic Coding / Lead Agents (`ma-2-lead-agents`)

> **Course:** MA (Agentic Horizon multi-agent series) · **Order:** 16 · **Volume:** vol-3-ma · **Chapter stem:** `16-lead-agents`
> **Video:** 34:56, native 4K (3840×2160). Public cut: `youtu.be/M30gp1315Y4` — "One Agent Is NOT ENOUGH: Agentic Coding BEYOND Claude Code."
> **Codebase:** members-only `lead-agents` (the multi-team-chat TOOL built on the **Pi coding agent** harness).

This file is the human-readable companion to `extract.json`. It carries the teaching scaffold (Hook → Picture → Worked example → Name → Failure → Retrieval) that the chapter is built from. Every technical claim is verified against the shipped codebase and the transcript; see `extract.json` → `confidence_notes` and `outdated-vs-current.md` for the version-drift flags.

---

## 0. The one-liner and the mission

**Tactic:** *One agent is not enough — build teams of agent experts.* Stop being the orchestrator. Replace the single, endlessly re-prompted agent with a **config-driven hierarchy of specialized, domain-locked, memory-compounding agent experts** that you command through **one** chat interface.

**Mission:** Move up the stack again — from *single-agent operator* to *commander of agent teams*. Dan's prediction: by the end of 2026 the engineer still re-prompting one agent in a terminal is the bottleneck (they **are** the orchestrator). You become irreplaceable by building a multi-team system where you type one message and 9 specialized agents across 3 teams decompose → build → validate while you watch. No babysitting. The endgame of TAC/Agentic Horizon: **systems that build systems**, so your codebase increasingly runs itself.

**Why it's uncomfortable:** it asks you to spend real money on coordination that *looks* wasteful. "Ping each team" is 7+ API calls; a full plan→engineer→validate cycle can cost ~$8 across Opus leads and Sonnet workers. Cost-min-maxers see the footer tick and call it waste. Dan's counter is the whole mindset of the lesson: *you are not thinking far enough into the future.* Intelligence-per-token rises while token cost falls, so the engineer who learns to **spend** those tokens on specialized teams **now** gets the asymmetric advantage.

> "One agent is not enough… today I want to show you a system that pushes beyond Claude Code. This is multi-team agentic coding. Here we use specialized teams of agents to outperform the normal distribution of results." — 0:06 / 1:02

---

## 1. Dan's spine: the 6 pillars

Dan frames the whole system as **6 pillars of multi-agent team orchestration** (from his own description). Use these as the chapter's backbone — every concept hangs off one:

1. **Team Leads & Workers** — a 3-tier hierarchy that removes YOU as the bottleneck.
2. **Expertise & Specialization** — specialized agents make expert decisions; generalists make mediocre ones.
3. **Agent Memory** — persistent per-agent mental models that compound session over session.
4. **Domain Ownership** — per-path boundary enforcement that makes full autonomy *safe* (blast radius ≈ zero).
5. **Chat Room Interface** — the coordination primitive; you talk only to the orchestrator.
6. **Configuration-Driven Harness** — the entire setup in one YAML, on the customizable Pi harness.

---

## 2. The architecture — depth-2 delegation

```
User
  │  (you type one message; you only ever talk to the Orchestrator)
  ▼
Orchestrator            model: claude-opus-4-6
  │  classify → tilldone list → delegate(team, question) → synthesize
  ├─▶ Planning Team
  │     ├─ Planning Lead      (opus) — routes to members, synthesizes; NEVER executes
  │     ├─ Product Manager    (sonnet)
  │     └─ UX Researcher      (sonnet)
  ├─▶ Engineering Team
  │     ├─ Engineering Lead   (opus)
  │     ├─ Frontend Dev       (sonnet)
  │     └─ Backend Dev        (sonnet)
  └─▶ Validation Team
        ├─ Validation Lead    (opus)
        ├─ QA Engineer        (sonnet)
        └─ Security Reviewer  (sonnet)
```

**10 agents total** = 1 orchestrator + 3 leads + 6 workers. (Dan's description says "2 teams, 3 members each, 9 agents" in one line — that's inconsistent with the shipped **3-team** default; the footer visibly renders 10 rows. Trust the code.)

**Delegation flow:** you → orchestrator → lead → worker; results bubble back up into one answer. Multi-team work chains **sequentially** (plan → engineer → validate), not in parallel — parallel delegation is the roadmap (see `outdated-vs-current.md`).

**Tier job matrix (the thing to memorize):**

| Tier | Model | Tools | Can run bash / mutate code? | Domain | Key skills |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Orchestrator | opus-4-6 | write, edit, read, grep, find, ls, **delegate**, **tilldone** | No | read all, write only its own expertise | conversational-response, zero-micro-management, active-listener, mental-model, **high-autonomy** |
| Team Lead | opus-4-6 | same as orchestrator | No | read all, write only its own mental-model file | conversational-response, zero-micro-management, active-listener, mental-model |
| Worker | sonnet-4-6 | read, write, edit, **bash**, grep, find, ls (no delegate/tilldone) | **Yes** | scoped per role | mental-model, active-listener, **precise-worker** |

The tier boundary is enforced *twice*: a **soft** layer (the `zero-micro-management` skill in the prompt) and a **hard** layer (domain rules + the `domain-enforcer` extension that structurally blocks out-of-domain tool calls). Both exist on purpose.

---

## 3. Teach each idea (weak-student scaffold)

### 3.1 One agent is a ceiling → teams of agents
- **Hook:** "If you're still re-prompting one agent over and over, YOU are the orchestrator, and that's the problem." You are doing the classify/route/synthesize work by hand.
- **Picture:** the ping demo (frame **t0118**) — you type `ping`, and only the Orchestrator answers: *"Pong. Teams are online — Planning, Engineering, Validation standing by."*
- **Worked example:** `ping each team lead` → the orchestrator fans out to all three leads and composes one reply; the footer sums Orchestrator + leads + context + total cost.
- **Name:** **multi-team agentic coding** — specialized *teams* (not just specialized agents) behind one interface.
- **Failure:** thinking this is "Claude Code subagents renamed." The unit here is the specialized **team** plus a single orchestrator; your cognitive load stays flat as you add specialists.
- **Retrieval:** *What makes you the bottleneck in single-agent coding, and what removes it?* (You are the orchestrator; a real orchestrator agent + team hierarchy removes it.)

### 3.2 The three tiers (depth-2 delegation)
- **Hook:** watch a lead try to do the work itself — and get *rejected*.
- **Picture:** frame **t0185** — the Engineering Lead's file tool calls show **red ✗** (domain-blocked); it says *"I have restricted tool access as a lead — let me delegate this to my team members who have the right tools,"* then `@Backend Dev` takes over. Three tiers visible in one screen.
- **Worked example:** "have engineering present a tree of the most important files" → Eng Lead blocked → delegates to Frontend + Backend Dev → they read the codebase → lead synthesizes → orchestrator reports.
- **Name:** **depth-2 delegation** — User → Orchestrator → Leads → Workers.
- **Failure:** assuming "deeper = better." Two levels is the sweet spot (OPUS.md); depth is hard-capped in code (a lead's `delegate` tool self-disables inside worker subprocesses).
- **Retrieval:** *Which tier runs bash and mutates code?* (Only workers.)

### 3.3 Leads delegate, never execute (zero micro-management)
- **Hook:** the lead is a *thinker*, not a *builder*.
- **Picture:** the `zero-micro-management` skill: "If you catch yourself about to use `write`, `edit`, or `bash` — stop and delegate instead."
- **Worked example:** `run head-to-head` → orchestrator can't do it → delegates to Engineering → the lead can't execute → passes to Backend Dev, who actually runs the eval.
- **Name:** **zero micro-management.**
- **Failure:** reading the lead's block as waste. It's the mechanism that routes work to the specialist with the right tools **and** the compounding domain memory.
- **Retrieval:** *Name the soft and hard enforcement of "leads don't execute."* (Soft: the skill. Hard: domain rules + the enforcer extension.)

### 3.4 Domain ownership / domain locking
- **Hook:** "No one should be touching the DevOps files except the DevOps agent."
- **Picture:** frame **t1290** — `frontend-dev.md` with a `domain:` block that lets it write to `apps/frontend/` but only **read** the rest of `apps` — the exact "read the back-end, never touch it" rule Dan types live.
- **Worked example:** each agent declares per-path `read` / `upsert` / `delete`. Planning leads read everything and write nothing but their own memory; QA/Security read code but never mutate it; backend/frontend devs write only their directories.
- **Name:** **domain locking** — "the holy grail of operating mid-to-large codebases."
- **Failure:** believing it's just a prompt instruction the model might ignore. It's a Pi extension (`domain-enforcer.ts`) that structurally blocks out-of-domain read/write/delete tool calls **and** heuristically blocks bash write/delete commands.
- **Retrieval:** *What three permissions does a domain rule grant, and is it prompt-only?* (read / upsert / delete; not prompt-only — structurally enforced.)

### 3.5 Agent experts + compounding memory
- **Hook:** "Agent experts will always outperform a generic agent."
- **Picture:** frames **t0232** (the specialized 10-agent roster) and **t1420** (the session log where the team wrote `head-to-head-results.md`).
- **Worked example:** each agent has an **expertise** YAML — its mental model. It reads it on boot, updates it after work, and the knowledge **compounds** every session (backend dev remembers backend patterns; QA remembers failure modes). You add read-only expertise (`updatable: false`) for billing / migrations / DevOps that nobody can corrupt.
- **Name:** **mental models / agent experts.**
- **Failure:** (1) thinking it's Claude-style "remember everything I ever said" — no, it's narrow, domain-specific, per-agent. (2) Micro-curating the files — Dan says *let go*; the `mental-model` skill is ~76 lines of guidance, and agents self-maintain within a `max-lines` cap.
- **Retrieval:** *In the shipped repo, what state are the expertise files in?* (Empty — 0 lines; agents build them per codebase. Dan's on-screen 5,000-token model was from a previous codebase.)

### 3.6 Composable skills
- **Hook:** change one line, change many agents.
- **Picture:** frame **t1180** — searching for `active-listener.md` returns **"10 results in 10 files"** — every agent shares that one skill.
- **Worked example:** `active-listener` (read the conversation log before every response) is shared by all 10; `conversational-response` (Slack-concise) is given to leads/orchestrator but withheld from workers so workers stay verbose; `zero-micro-management` goes only to leads/orchestrator.
- **Name:** **skills composition** — the control surface.
- **Failure:** treating skills as inert prompt snippets. They're how you retune the whole fleet cheaply: "There could be an agent somewhere that we don't want to always active-listen. We want it to be independent."
- **Retrieval:** *Which skill do all 10 agents share, and which is withheld from workers (and why)?* (Shared: active-listener. Withheld: conversational-response — workers stay verbose/detailed.)

### 3.7 Configuration-driven teams
- **Hook:** "Really is as simple as updating the configuration file."
- **Picture:** the `multi-team-config.yaml` roster + the file tree (`.pi/multi-team/agents|expertise|skills|sessions`).
- **Worked example:** kill the app, edit the config to drop the Validation team, relaunch → "now it's just planning and engineering." Add a DevOps team by copying the pattern.
- **Name:** **config-driven teams** on the **customizable Pi harness** (`.pi` vs `.claude`).
- **Failure:** assuming domain/skills/expertise live in the YAML. They **don't** — the YAML holds only the roster + `consult-when` routing; each agent's model/tools/skills/expertise/domain live in that agent's `.md` frontmatter. The repo ships 5 preset configs (default, engineering-only, planning+engineering, plan + 3× scaled engineering, and an open-source multi-provider config).
- **Retrieval:** *Where does an agent's domain live — the config or the agent file?* (The agent `.md` frontmatter.)

### 3.8 Model tiering + spend to win
- **Hook:** "$8 for this agent team?!"
- **Picture:** frame **t1610** — real ML results delivered by the team, footer showing **Orch $8.280** total, Opus leads / Sonnet workers.
- **Worked example:** leads/orchestrator on Opus (coordination needs the best intelligence), workers on Sonnet (follow instructions + execute). Overridable per agent via config `model:` (the `opensource` config points 9 agents at 9 different provider models).
- **Name:** **model tiering + spend-to-win.**
- **Failure:** fixating on cost. Dan: *"You're thinking about right now. You're not thinking far enough into the future… I am not spending enough tokens, you are not spending enough tokens, and you are wasting time doing things the old way."*
- **Retrieval:** *What's Dan's answer to "this is expensive"?* (Spend more; cost/intelligence is moving in your favor; the old single-agent way is finished.)

### 3.9 Conversation awareness + session workspace
- **Hook:** "Every agent is aware of the conversation happening."
- **Picture:** frame **t1420** — the actual `conversation.jsonl` (rows with `from`/`message`/`to`/`type`/`timestamp`) plus the per-session directory with per-team folders and the artifacts the team wrote.
- **Worked example:** copy the session ID → open `sessions/<id>/conversation.jsonl` → read the entire multi-agent exchange. The `active-listener` skill makes every agent load recent history before responding.
- **Name:** **conversation awareness** + the **session directory** as shared workspace.
- **Failure:** thinking each agent is a private black box. The shared log is what keeps the team coordinated and observable.
- **Retrieval:** *What file makes the whole team's exchange observable, and what skill uses it?* (`conversation.jsonl`; the active-listener skill.)

### 3.10 Trust and scale (the 2026 close)
- **Hook:** "The name of the game in 2026 is trust and scale."
- **Picture:** frame **t0955** — the validation team autonomously reviews and returns "Ship it. No blocking issues."
- **Worked example:** raise trust until you *know* the team ships on enter; then scale — how big, how surgical. Compounding, domain-locked agent experts drive both.
- **Name:** **trust → scale.**
- **Failure:** thinking trust comes from watching more closely. It comes from building specialized, domain-locked, memory-compounding experiences and then letting go.
- **Retrieval:** *What are the two moves of 2026, in order?* (Trust, then scale.)

---

## 4. The worked demo (the classifier)

The teams operate **on** a separate **prompt-routing / prompt-complexity-classifier** codebase (data-science / scikit-learn). **It is NOT shipped** in the lead-agents repo — Dan deploys the multi-team tool *into* it via the `teams` alias. The classifier's job: predict whether a prompt needs a **low / mid / high** intelligence model so you "don't pay for intelligence you don't need."

The thread you can point at, frame by frame:
1. `just predict "summarize this codebase"` → **Label: MID**, confidences `{high 0.35, low 0.19, mid 0.45}`, routes to `claude-sonnet-4-6` (frame **t0337**).
2. "Ask all teams: two more scikit-learn classifiers to benchmark" → the orchestrator writes the **same** domain-tailored prompt to all 3 teams → consensus (LinearSVC, CalibratedClassifierCV; Engineering adds SGDClassifier; ComplementNB as challenger) (frame **t0408**).
3. "plan, engineer, then validate" → Planning drafts → Backend Dev implements the benchmark → QA + Security review → Security "ship it," QA finds extra items (frame **t0955**).
4. `run head-to-head` → LR owns LOW (80% vs 67%), ComplementNB owns MID (86% vs 76%), both perfect on HIGH; "LR stays as default, CNB a credible challenger"; results written to `sessions/<id>/head-to-head-results.md` (frame **t1610**).

Design nuance worth teaching: the classifier is deliberately **biased toward predicting HIGH** as a safe default — mispredicting LOW/MID when you truly needed HIGH is the costly, user-hurting error.

---

## 5. How it actually works (the harness internals)

For the chapter's "under the hood" box — all read from source (`apps/multi-team-chat/extensions/`), corroborated by the two specs:

- **The nuclear UI pattern.** `multi-team-chat.ts` (1,432 lines) doesn't theme Pi — it *replaces* Pi's display layer: inside `session_start` it reassigns `tui.children[1] = chatView` (custom chat) and blanks `[2]`/`[3]`. The agent brain stays; the whole UI is Dan's.
- **Delegation = subprocesses.** Each tier is the *same* `pi` binary spawned as a subprocess: `callAgent()` runs `pi --mode json -p --no-extensions -e domain-enforcer -e lead-delegate -e tilldone-lead --no-skills --no-prompt-templates --no-themes --session <file>` (and, first run only, `--system-prompt`/`--model`). Sessions are persistent JSONL — the system prompt is set once and resumed after.
- **The env-var handshake caps depth.** Three extensions load on *every* subprocess and each self-exits unless its trigger env var is present: `domain-enforcer` (`AGENT_DOMAIN_RULES`), `lead-delegate` (`LEAD_MEMBERS_JSON`), `tilldone-lead` (`TILLDONE_TASKS`). A lead deletes `LEAD_MEMBERS_JSON` before spawning members → members can't re-delegate. That's the hard depth cap.
- **`delegate` and `tilldone` are custom Pi tools.** The orchestrator's `delegate(teams, question)` accepts a team name, a list, or `"all"`; it runs `Promise.allSettled` over the targets and streams each lead's output into a live chat bubble. `tilldone` gives the orchestrator a visible task list; leads get a reduced `start`/`done`/`list` variant.
- **The domain enforcer is structural.** `domain-enforcer.ts` blocks `tool_call`s: an allowlist (`AGENT_ALLOWED_TOOLS`), per-path `isAllowed(path, read|upsert)` (expertise files first, then directory rules; `.` root is special; *no matching rule = deny*), and bash write/delete regex heuristics. On `tool_result` it re-reads expertise files and warns if over `max-lines`.
- **Runtime variable injection.** `config.ts`/`prompt.ts` fill `{{SESSION_DIR}}`, `{{CONVERSATION_LOG}}`, `{{TEAMS_BLOCK}}`/`{{MEMBERS_BLOCK}}`, `{{EXPERTISE_BLOCK}}`, `{{SKILLS_BLOCK}}` into each system prompt at startup — this is the "controlling the core four down to the system-prompt level" Dan describes.
- **Two orchestration models coexist — don't conflate them.** The Pi **runtime** uses `delegate`/`tilldone` + `pi` subprocesses (the lesson's subject). The `.claude` tooling (`plan-w-team.md`, `meta-pi-agent.md`, and the specs' "Team Orchestration / Task*" sections) uses the **Claude Code Task\*** tools to *build* the system. Claude Code is the **bootloader** (`/install`, `/install-into-codebase`); Pi is the runtime.

Two real engineering artifacts make excellent "this is real, not a demo" material:
- `specs/fix-worker-cost-tracking.md` — a real bug: worker costs were silently dropped, so the footer showed **$8.79 vs a real $34.28** (~25% of spend). Three-layer fix. Perfect "spend to win, *but track your spend*" tension.
- `specs/tilldone-multi-team-integration.md` — the full plan for adding the task-tracking system (parent canonical state, lead mutations via `onToolUpdate`, two-column footer, `agent_end` nudge).

---

## 6. Codebase orientation (what the student is looking at)

The shipped repo **is the tool.** Highest-value files to read, in order:
1. `README.md` — the definitive written companion (why multi-team, setup, architecture, memory, domain, skills, config, tilldone, conversation awareness, emoji legend).
2. `.pi/multi-team/multi-team-config.yaml` — the roster.
3. `.pi/multi-team/agents/orchestrator.md` → `planning-lead.md` → `backend-dev.md` — read one of each tier and diff the frontmatter (model / tools / skills / domain).
4. `.pi/multi-team/skills/*.md` — the 6 composable behaviors.
5. `OPUS.md` — architecture reflections + roadmap (parallel delegation).
6. `apps/multi-team-chat/extensions/` — the harness internals (see §5).
7. `justfile` — how to launch (`just team`, `team-eng`, `team-pe`, `team-scale`, `team-oss`).

Full file-by-file map in `extract.json` → `codebase_map`.

---

## 7. Pointers
- **Quotable insights & distilled ideas:** `nuggets.md`
- **Version drift + current-practice notes:** `outdated-vs-current.md`
- **Gradable lab (course codebase only):** `lab-spec.md`
- **Frames (11, all 4K, verified on disk):** `frame-plan.json` and `media/frames/ma-2-lead-agents/`
- **YouTube overlays (2 phase-1, real IDs from the lesson's own description):** `extract.json` → `yt_overlay_candidates`
FILE: IndyDevDan/book/_extracts/ma-2-lead-agents/nuggets.md
# Nuggets — Multi-Team Agentic Coding / Lead Agents (`ma-2-lead-agents`)

Distilled, teachable ideas from the lesson — the "loot box" of insights. Each is a one-line hook + a short expansion grounded in the transcript, `README.md`, `OPUS.md`, or the source. Use them for pull-quotes, section epigraphs, and retrieval prompts.

---

## The thesis

1. **One agent is a ceiling.** No single agent, however smart, escapes it. The upgrade isn't a smarter agent — it's a *team* of specialized agents behind one interface.

2. **If you re-prompt one agent all day, YOU are the orchestrator.** That's the bottleneck. The whole system exists to move the classify/route/synthesize work off of you and onto an orchestrator agent.

3. **Outperform the normal distribution of results.** Specialized teams don't just match a single agent's average output — they push past it. That's the entire value proposition, and it's why it's "not for cost-min-maxers."

4. **This is agentic coding *beyond* Claude Code.** Multi-agent orchestration is today's frontier; multi-team agentic coding on a fully customizable harness is the next step past it.

## Architecture

5. **Depth-2 delegation maps to a real org.** User → Orchestrator → Team Leads → Workers. Two levels of delegation, no more. "Most systems either go flat or go too deep — two levels hits the sweet spot." (OPUS.md)

6. **You only ever talk to the orchestrator.** That single interface is what keeps *your* cognitive load flat as the number of specialists grows. Adding a team is a config edit, not a new thing for you to manage.

7. **Leads think; workers do.** Leads and the orchestrator *route, coordinate, and synthesize* — they never write, edit, or run bash. "You're a leader — delegate, never execute."

8. **The block is the feature.** When the Engineering Lead gets rejected from touching files, that's not waste — it forces the work to the specialist who has the right tools *and* the compounding domain memory.

9. **Only the workers touch the code.** Workers are the sole tier with `bash` + write/edit. That's what makes the hierarchy safe: coordination and execution are physically separated.

## Domain & safety

10. **Domain locking is the holy grail of large codebases.** Per-agent `read` / `upsert` / `delete` permissions per path. "No one should be touching the DevOps files except the DevOps agent."

11. **Two locks, not one.** A *soft* lock (the `zero-micro-management` skill in the prompt) and a *hard* lock (the `domain-enforcer` extension that structurally blocks out-of-domain tool calls and bash writes). The prompt can be argued with; the enforcer cannot.

12. **Boundary enforcement makes autonomy safe.** You can hand an agent full autonomy precisely *because* its blast radius is near zero. Autonomy without domain locking is how agents step on each other's files.

13. **No matching rule = deny.** The enforcer's default is closed: if nothing in the domain grants access to a path, the tool call is blocked. Safe by construction.

## Expertise & memory

14. **Agent experts always beat generic agents.** Same reason you trust the teammate who's shipped it a dozen times over the new hire. Specialization compounds; generality plateaus.

15. **Agents that remember are a competitive advantage over agents that forget.** Every agent keeps a personal mental-model file it reads on boot and updates after work. Session over session, knowledge stacks.

16. **This is NOT "remember everything I ever said."** Dan is explicit: global chat memory "is not going to go well." Specialized agents with *specific purposes* building *domain-relevant* memory — that's what goes well.

17. **You can't have your fingers on everything.** The journey to becoming an agentic engineer is learning to *let go* — teach the agent how to build a good mental model (the skill is ~76 lines), then don't touch the file.

18. **Read-only expertise is a load-bearing pattern.** `updatable: false` gives a billing/migrations/DevOps agent a playbook no one can corrupt — highly opinionated knowledge you never want an agent to "improve."

19. **Empty by default, rich over time.** The shipped repo ships all 10 mental models *empty*. They're meant to be grown per-codebase — the value is in the compounding, not the starter file.

## Skills & config

20. **Skills are the control surface.** Compose small markdown behaviors; share one to change many agents, remove one line to change one. `active-listener` lives in all 10 agents; `conversational-response` is deliberately withheld from workers so they stay verbose.

21. **The whole team is one YAML.** Add a team, prune a team, swap a member — one file, picked up on next launch. Colors, routing hints, roster. (But model/tools/skills/domain live in each agent's `.md`, not the config.)

22. **Config is where you scale horizontally.** The `plan-and-scale` preset runs *three parallel Engineering teams* from the same agent definitions — team count is a config knob.

23. **The model tier is a knob too.** Config `model:` overrides frontmatter `model:`. The `opensource` preset points 9 agents at 9 different providers — one interface, many brains.

## The mindset (spend, trust, scale)

24. **Spend to win.** "We are not afraid to spend to win here. We're not afraid to give our agents all the relevant context they need." 1M-token windows make deep specialization + memory viable — *if* you're willing to use the tokens.

25. **The $8 objection is a failure of imagination.** "You're thinking about right now. You're not thinking far enough into the future. Models will improve, costs are going down." The complaint dates you.

26. **You are not spending enough tokens.** Dan says it about himself too. The scarce resource isn't tokens — it's the willingness to reallocate work to compute.

27. **Where the ball is going, not where it is.** Build for the converging trends (rising intelligence, bigger context windows, cheaper tokens, customizable harnesses), not for today's constraints.

28. **2026 is trust and scale.** First raise trust until you *know* the team ships the result when you hit enter. Then scale — how big, how surgical. In that order.

29. **Systems that build systems.** The payoff of agentic coding: agents that plan, build, test, and validate your application so you compound your advantage instead of typing.

## Under the hood (for the "this is real" box)

30. **The nuclear UI pattern.** Three array assignments replace Pi's entire display layer while keeping the agent brain intact — the kind of hack only possible on a fully customizable harness.

31. **Every tier is the same binary, spawned as a subprocess.** `pi --mode json -p` with three extensions loaded; each self-exits unless its trigger env var is present. Elegant and uniform.

32. **Depth is capped by deleting an env var.** A lead strips `LEAD_MEMBERS_JSON` before spawning members, so the members' copy of the delegate tool self-disables. That's the whole recursion guard.

33. **The hardest problem is routing.** "Everything downstream only matters if the right team gets the right question." (OPUS.md) Teaching the orchestrator *when to delegate, what to delegate, and when to just answer* is the skill that compounds.

34. **The next frontier is parallel delegation.** `Promise.allSettled` already wraps the loop; the hard part is teaching the orchestrator when work is parallel-safe vs dependent. "Plan then build — sequential. Review from two angles — parallel."

35. **Track your spend or the footer lies.** The real `fix-worker-cost-tracking` bug: the TUI showed $8.79 while the API dashboard showed $34.28 — worker costs were silently dropped. Spend to win, *and* measure what you spend.
FILE: IndyDevDan/book/_extracts/ma-2-lead-agents/outdated-vs-current.md
# Outdated vs Current — Multi-Team Agentic Coding / Lead Agents (`ma-2-lead-agents`)

What in this lesson is **period detail** (a specific tool/model/UI state at recording time) versus what is **timeless** (the tactic). Dan future-proofs the model/UI drift himself in-lesson — treat specifics as a snapshot, tactics as current. Written against the ecosystem as of **mid-2026**; the model-name and harness-capability axes move fast, so re-verify at publish.

Two kinds of drift are documented here:
- **A. Recording-vs-shipped drift** — where the video shows an *earlier* state than the checked-in repo (verified by reading both).
- **B. Ecosystem drift** — where the wider tooling has moved since recording.

---

## A. Recording-vs-shipped drift (verify against the shipped files, not the video)

### A1. The on-screen `orchestrator.md` predates the TillDone integration
- **In the video** (frame **t0768**), `orchestrator.md` shows `tools: read, grep, find, ls, delegate` and only **two** domain entries (`.pi/multi-team/`, `.`).
- **In the shipped file**, the orchestrator has `tools: write, edit, read, grep, find, ls, delegate, **tilldone**` and **three** domain entries (adds `.pi/multi-team/expertise/` with `upsert: true`).
- **Why:** `tilldone` was added *after* this recording — `specs/tilldone-multi-team-integration.md` is the plan that added it (orchestrator + the three leads gained the tool; workers deliberately did not). The video captures a mid-development state.
- **Teaching guidance:** teach from the **shipped** files. If you show frame t0768, caption it as "an earlier build — the shipped orchestrator also has `write/edit/tilldone` and can write its own expertise."

### A2. The front-end/back-end domain SPLIT is a live demo edit, not the shipped default
- **In the video** (frame **t1290**), Dan edits `frontend-dev.md` so its domain scopes writes to `apps/frontend/` (read+upsert+delete) while only **reading** the rest of `apps` — the "read the back-end, never touch it" rule — and shows the mirror-image edit for `backend-dev.md`.
- **In the shipped files**, `frontend-dev.md` and `backend-dev.md` have **identical** app-domain: `apps/` with read+upsert+delete. There is no `apps/frontend/` / `apps/backend/` split checked in.
- **Why:** the shipped repo is a "simple example" (a data-science, backend-focused target; Dan even notes the front-end dev isn't really needed for prompt-routing). The split is Dan demonstrating *what you would do* on a real front/back codebase.
- **Teaching guidance:** present the split as the *pattern to apply*, and be explicit that the shipped default is unsplit. Great "you try" lab step (see `lab-spec.md` S3).

### A3. The mental-model (expertise) files ship EMPTY
- **In the video**, the back-end dev's mental model is ~5,000 tokens of rich, structured notes (specs, missing infra, key risks, backend patterns, security, testing).
- **In the shipped repo**, all **10** expertise files are **0 lines** (verified with `wc -l`).
- **Why:** that on-screen model was Dan's *working copy from a previous codebase* — he says on camera, "I actually need to get rid of this and tune the mental model… it's got information stored about its previous codebase." `/install-into-codebase` creates **empty** expertise; agents grow their own per codebase.
- **Teaching guidance:** the compounding is the point, not the starter file. Weak students who open an empty file and think "it's broken" are missing that these are meant to fill in over sessions.

### A4. Dan's own description says "2 teams" — the shipped default is 3
- The video description says "9 specialized agents across 3 teams" in one place and "2 teams, 3 members each, 9 agents" in another.
- The shipped `multi-team-config.yaml` and the footer both show **3 teams / 10 agents** (1 orchestrator + 3 leads + 6 workers).
- **Teaching guidance:** use the code's numbers (3 teams, 10 agents). The "2 teams" line is a description inconsistency, not the system.

---

## B. Ecosystem drift (period detail vs timeless tactic)

### B1. Model names (`claude-opus-4-6`, `claude-sonnet-4-6`, "Haiku")
- **Period detail:** the agent frontmatter pins `anthropic/claude-opus-4-6` (orchestrator + leads) and `anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6` (workers); the transcript names "Opus 4.6," "Sonnet 4.6," and "Claude Haiku." The `opensource` config names then-current third-party models (qwen3.5, kimi-k2.5, gemini-3-flash-preview, gpt-5.4/-mini, minimax-m2.7, mimo-v2-pro).
- **Timeless tactic:** **tier your models** — best intelligence where coordination happens (orchestrator + leads), cheaper where you just execute (workers) — or go all-Opus if you're maxing out. Dan blesses drift directly: models will improve; that's great; don't let it distract you.
- **Action at publish:** update model ids to the current tier equivalents; keep the tiering principle verbatim.

### B2. Sequential delegation only; parallel is the roadmap
- **Now:** the orchestrator delegates **one team at a time** (plan → engineer → validate). Dependent work is exactly right for this.
- **Coming:** `OPUS.md` names **parallel delegation** as the next direction — `Promise.allSettled` already wraps the delegation loop; the hard part is teaching the orchestrator *when* work is parallel-safe vs dependent ("review this PR" → Engineering + Validation at once). Independent cross-team requests are the near-term unlock, not the current default.
- **Teaching guidance:** tell the reader the ceiling is higher than what the video shows; don't imply parallel fan-out is already the behavior.

### B3. Two orchestration models coexist — don't conflate them
- **The Pi RUNTIME** (the lesson's subject) uses custom `delegate` / `tilldone` tools and spawns `pi` subprocesses. This is what runs when you type in the chat room.
- **The Claude Code BUILD harness**: the `.claude/` tooling — `plan-w-team.md`, `meta-pi-agent.md`, and the "Team Orchestration / Task*" sections of both specs — uses Claude Code's **Task\*** tools (`TaskCreate`/`TaskUpdate`/`Task`/`TaskOutput`) to *build and extend* the system. Claude Code is the **bootloader** (`/install`, `/install-into-codebase`).
- **Ecosystem drift:** Claude Code's own subagent/Task/background-task and Agent-Skills capabilities have grown since recording, which makes it *easier* to conflate the build harness with the runtime. Keep the two layers distinct in the chapter.

### B4. "Beyond Claude Code" — harness customization is less exotic now
- **The lesson's premise:** the Pi harness is customizable down to files/folders/tools/hooks/**UI** ("the nuclear UI pattern"), which is what lets Dan build a multi-team chat room. He notes you *can* build a more limited version in Claude Code via subagents + hooks ("Pi has the exact same system" of hooks).
- **Drift:** since recording, Claude Code and peers have expanded their own customization surfaces (Agent Skills with `SKILL.md`, hooks, output styles, plugins, settings layering). The **ideas** — teams, leads, domain locking, compounding memory, a single chat-room interface — now have more than one viable home.
- **Timeless tactic:** don't marry the tool. Dan: "More tools like this will emerge, but the ideas will be there." Anchor the chapter on the transferable architecture, not on Pi specifically.

### B5. Multi-agent tooling landscape
- The description name-drops **CMUX** alongside Claude Code as multi-agent tooling "here now." The specific tool roster will keep changing; the point ("multi-agent orchestration is the frontier right now, and the engineers who build these patterns get the asymmetric advantage") is durable.

---

## What is fully timeless (state these plainly)

- **One agent is a ceiling; teams of agent experts break it.**
- **Depth-2 delegation** (User → Orchestrator → Leads → Workers) with a **single orchestrator interface**.
- **Leads delegate, never execute**, enforced both softly (skill) and structurally (domain rules).
- **Domain ownership** (per-path read/upsert/delete) as the unlock for safe autonomy at scale.
- **Agent experts > generalists**, powered by **compounding, domain-scoped mental models** you let the agents self-maintain.
- **Composable skills** as the fleet-wide control surface.
- **Configuration-driven teams** — the whole roster in one file.
- **Model tiering + spend-to-win**, and the **2026 game of trust then scale.**

---

## Current-practice overlays (YouTube)

Both phase-1 overlays are sourced **directly from the lesson's own description** (real IDs, oembed-verify before publishing):
- **`f8cfH5XX-XU` — "Pi vs Claude Code"** → the named "beyond Claude Code / customizable harness / hooks" gap (Dan references it in-lesson).
- **`TqjmTZRL31E` — "CEO Agents"** → the immediately-prior lesson this one builds on (context / 1M window / multi-agent foundation).

See `extract.json` → `yt_overlay_candidates` for the full list and rationale (plus the phase-2 candidates).
FILE: IndyDevDan/book/_extracts/ma-2-lead-agents/lab-spec.md
# Lab — Multi-Team Agentic Coding / Lead Agents (`ma-2-lead-agents`)

**Goal:** operate, inspect, and reconfigure the multi-team system until you can explain the 3-tier architecture, domain locking, skills composition, and model tiering **without notes** — and prove each with evidence you produced.

## Scope contract (read first)
- **Course codebase only.** The shipped `lead-agents` repo **is the tool** (a Pi extension + `.pi/multi-team/` team definitions). The scikit-learn *prompt-routing classifier* codebase Dan operates on in the video is **NOT shipped** — so this lab runs the tool **against its own source repo** (exactly what `README.md` → "Step 2: Test Locally" prescribes). Do not try to reproduce the classifier benchmark.
- **No PiCanvas. No destructive Git/`rm` improvisation.** Restore any file you edit.
- **This costs tokens.** Even a light session can run several dollars. Have `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` funded or a Claude plan. Keep test prompts small.
- Every checkpoint below is **gradable** — it has an explicit, checkable pass condition.

---

## Setup

1. **Prereqs on PATH:** Claude Code (`claude`), Pi (`pi` or `ipi`), `bun`, `node`, `just`. Confirm: `claude --version`, `pi --version` (or `ipi --version`), `bun --version`, `just --version`.
2. **`.env`:** create `.env` from `.env.sample`; set `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...` (required). Optional provider keys only if you'll run the multi-provider config.
3. **Install:** from the repo root run `/install` inside Claude Code (or `just install`). It checks prereqs, verifies `.env`, runs `bun install`, validates the config + agent files, and adds a shell alias (default `teams`) to your profile. Reload your shell.
4. **Say the permissions out loud before launching:** open `.pi/multi-team/agents/orchestrator.md` and read its `domain:` block. What can the orchestrator read? What can it write? (Answer: read everything; write only its own expertise.)

---

## Checkpoint 0 — Launch + ping (the single interface)
- Run `just team` (default 3-team config against this repo). Wait for the chat room to boot.
- Type `ping`.
- **Pass:** only the **Orchestrator** responds (a pong-style message confirming Planning/Engineering/Validation are online), and the footer shows an Orchestrator line with cost + context tokens. You did **not** talk to any worker directly. *(Matches frame t0118.)*

## Checkpoint 1 — Three-tier delegation + a domain block
- Type: `ping each team lead`. Observe the two-layer fan-out (Orchestrator → 3 leads) and the footer summing lead costs.
- Then type: `have engineering present a tree structure of the most important files in this repo`.
- **Pass:** you observe the **Engineering Lead** attempt file access, get **domain-blocked** (visible ✗ / "restricted tool access as a lead"), and **delegate to a worker** (Backend/Frontend Dev) that actually reads the tree — three tiers in one exchange, results synthesized back up. *(Matches frame t0185.)*
- **Record:** one sentence — *why* did the lead delegate instead of reading the files itself?

## Checkpoint 2 — Reveal the full team (cost + model tiering)
- Type `/toggle-workers`.
- **Pass:** the footer/tree shows **all 10 agents** (Orch + 3 leads + 6 workers), each with its own **cost** and **model**; you can point to **Opus for orchestrator+leads** and **Sonnet for workers**. *(Matches frames t0232 / t1610.)*
- **Record:** the per-agent model tier and the total (orchestrator) cost at this moment.

## Checkpoint 3 — Multi-perspective (consistent fan-out)
- Ask a repo-relevant question of all teams, e.g.: `ask all teams: what is the single biggest risk in this codebase's architecture, and why?`
- **Pass:** the Orchestrator delegates the **same** (domain-tailored) question to all three teams and returns **one** synthesized answer noting consensus/splits — you never addressed a team directly. *(Matches frame t0408.)*

## Checkpoint 4 — Read the anatomy (where the config lives)
- Open and compare `orchestrator.md`, `planning-lead.md`, and `backend-dev.md` frontmatter, plus `.pi/multi-team/multi-team-config.yaml` and two skills (`zero-micro-management.md`, `active-listener.md`).
- **Pass:** you can answer, from the files:
  1. Which tier has `bash` and which does not? *(Workers do; leads/orchestrator don't.)*
  2. Where does an agent's **model / tools / skills / domain** live — the YAML or the agent `.md`? *(The agent `.md` frontmatter; the YAML holds only the roster + `consult-when`.)*
  3. Which skill is shared by **all 10** agents, and which is **withheld from workers** (and why)? *(Shared: `active-listener`. Withheld: `conversational-response` — workers stay verbose.)*

## Checkpoint 5 — Conversation awareness (the shared log)
- Find the current session's directory under `.pi/multi-team/sessions/<session-id>/` and open `conversation.jsonl`.
- **Pass:** you can see rows from **every tier** (user, orchestrator, leads, members) with `from`/`message`/`type`, proving every agent can load the shared conversation. *(Matches frame t1420.)*

---

## Stretch drills

### S1 — Prune a team (config-driven)
Kill the app. Either run `just team-pe` (Planning + Engineering preset) **or** copy `multi-team-config.yaml`, delete the Validation team block, and launch it with `just teamc <your-config>`. **Pass:** the relaunched roster shows **only** the remaining teams (no Validation lead/workers in the tree).

### S2 — Add a team (config-driven)
In a copy of the config, add a new team block (e.g. a "DevOps" team) pointing its lead/members at existing agent `.md` files (reuse engineering-lead + backend-dev is fine for the drill), give it a distinct color and `consult-when`. Launch with `just teamc <your-config>`. **Pass:** the Orchestrator lists/uses the new team when you ask something in its domain.

### S3 — Domain-lock experiment (the hard gate)
Edit `frontend-dev.md`'s `domain:` to scope writes to `apps/frontend/` only (read-only on the rest of `apps/`), mirroring the video (frame t1290). Relaunch, then via the Orchestrator ask Engineering to have the **Frontend Dev** write a file **outside** `apps/frontend/`. **Pass:** the `domain-enforcer` **blocks** the write (you see a refusal/blocked tool call), demonstrating the hard gate is structural, not prompt-only. **Restore** `frontend-dev.md` afterward.

### S4 — Model override precedence
Run `just team-oss` (multi-provider preset) **or** add a config-level `model:` to one agent in a config copy. **Pass:** that agent runs on the **overridden** model (visible in the tree), proving config `model:` beats the agent's frontmatter `model:`. *(Requires the relevant provider key in `.env` for `team-oss`.)*

### S5 — Read-only expertise
Add a second expertise entry to one worker with `updatable: false` (e.g. a "deploy-checklist.yaml" with a couple of lines) and reference it in `use-when`. Ask that agent to modify that file. **Pass:** the agent can **read** it but is **blocked from writing** it (read-only expertise is enforced by the enforcer's expertise check). Alternatively, observe `max-lines` enforcement by inspecting the `mental-model` skill's line-limit rule. Restore afterward.

### S6 — Scale horizontally (advanced)
Run `just team-scale` (Planning + **3 parallel Engineering teams**). **Pass:** the tree shows Engineering1/2/3, each with its own lead + FE + BE — team count is a config knob. (Watch the cost; keep the prompt trivial.)

### S7 — Meta build (advanced, optional)
Boot the meta agent with `just metacc` (Claude Code) or `just metapi` (Pi) and ask it to scaffold one new specialized agent + wire it into a config. **Pass:** a new agent `.md` + config entry that launches. *(This exercises the Claude Code build harness — distinct from the Pi runtime.)*

---

## Pass criteria (final sheet — all must be true)
1. The system is installed and launches (`just team` / your `teams` alias); `ping` returns an **Orchestrator-only** response with cost in the footer.
2. You **witnessed a lead get domain-blocked and delegate to a worker** (the three-tier cascade) and can explain why in one sentence.
3. `/toggle-workers` showed **all 10 agents** with per-agent cost and the **Opus-leads / Sonnet-workers** tiering; you recorded the tiers and total cost.
4. You **reconfigured the roster via YAML** (pruned S1 or added S2 a team) and relaunched successfully.
5. You **triggered a `domain-enforcer` block** (S3) by asking an agent to write outside its domain, then **restored** the file.
6. You opened `conversation.jsonl` and confirmed **every tier's** messages are logged.
7. Without notes you can state: the **3 tiers + what each may/may not do**; **where** model/tools/skills/domain live (agent frontmatter, not the config); the **soft vs hard** enforcement of "leads don't execute"; and **Dan's answer to the cost objection** (spend more; cost/intelligence trends favor you).

---

## Troubleshooting (keyed to the real mechanics)
| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| "Ghost" / duplicate messages when re-testing | Reused a live Pi/tmux session | Per `CLAUDE.md`: fully kill + recreate the session between runs; wait for the orchestrator to go idle before sending the next prompt. Prefer bun over npm. |
| Launch dies immediately | Missing `.env` or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` | `config.ts` `loadDotEnv` **throws** if `.env` is absent. Create it from `.env.sample` and set the key. |
| "FATAL"/crash naming an agent file | An agent `.md` referenced by the config is missing/renamed | `loadAgentConfig` throws on a missing agent file. Fix the path in your config or restore the `.md`. |
| A worker "refuses" / a write silently doesn't happen | The `domain-enforcer` blocked an out-of-domain tool call | Check that agent's `domain:` block. This is the feature (S3), not a bug. |
| Footer cost looks suspiciously low | Worker-cost propagation edge (see `specs/fix-worker-cost-tracking.md`) | Costs are captured across three layers; if a build predates that fix, worker costs can under-report (~25%). Cross-check against your API dashboard. |
| A lead tries to write code and stalls | Working as designed | Leads have no execution domain — they must `delegate`. This is the three-tier boundary. |
| Provider agent (team-oss) errors | Missing provider key | Add the relevant `OPENROUTER_/OPENAI_/GEMINI_` key to `.env`, or use the default Anthropic config. |
