  "retrieval_questions": [
    {
      "q": "What are the three tiers, and what is each tier's job?",
      "a": "Orchestrator (classify + route + synthesize; the only interface you talk to), Team Leads (coordinate + delegate to members + synthesize; never execute), Workers (read/write/bash within their domain). Depth-2 delegation: User -> Orchestrator -> Leads -> Workers."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which tier can run bash and mutate code, and which cannot?",
      "a": "Only workers have bash + write/edit and mutate code. Orchestrator and leads have delegate + tilldone (and write/edit scoped to their own memory) — they coordinate, they don't execute."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does domain locking control, and is it just a prompt instruction?",
      "a": "Per-path read / upsert / delete permissions per agent. It is NOT just a prompt — the domain-enforcer Pi extension hard-blocks out-of-domain read/write/delete tool calls and heuristically blocks bash write/delete commands. The zero-micro-management skill is the soft layer on top."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does an agent's model, tools, skills, and domain live — the YAML config or the agent file?",
      "a": "In the agent's .md frontmatter. The multi-team-config.yaml only holds the roster (orchestrator, teams, leads, members, colors, consult-when routing)."
    },
    {
      "q": "What model tier runs the orchestrator + leads vs the workers?",
      "a": "Orchestrator + leads = anthropic/claude-opus-4-6 (best intelligence for coordination). Workers = anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6 (follow instructions + execute). Overridable per agent via config model:."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a mental model / expertise file, who maintains it, and what's its shipped state?",
      "a": "A per-agent YAML mental model the agent reads on boot and updates after work, compounding session over session (max-lines capped). The agent self-maintains it; you don't touch it. In the shipped repo all 10 are EMPTY — agents build them per-codebase."
    },
    {
      "q": "Name three shared skills and who gets them.",
      "a": "active-listener (all 10 agents — read the conversation log before every response); zero-micro-management (orchestrator + leads only); conversational-response (orchestrator + leads, withheld from workers so workers stay verbose). Also mental-model (all), high-autonomy (orchestrator), precise-worker (workers)."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do you add or remove a team?",
      "a": "Edit the single multi-team-config.yaml (add/delete a team block or swap a member path); the orchestrator picks it up on next launch. Preset configs and `just` recipes exist for common shapes (team-eng, team-pe, team-scale, team-oss)."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the Engineering Lead delegate instead of reading the files itself?",
      "a": "Leads are domain-scoped to read/write only their own memory and are told 'delegate, never execute.' When it tries file tools it is domain-blocked (visible red X's), so it routes the work to Backend/Frontend Dev who have the tools and the compounding domain memory."
    },
    {
      "q": "What keeps your cognitive load flat as you add more specialists?",
      "a": "The single orchestrator interface + chat room. You only ever talk to the orchestrator; adding specialists is a config edit, and workers are never addressed directly."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the conversation.jsonl file and why does it matter?",
      "a": "A shared append-only log of every message from every tier (with tool calls). Combined with the active-listener skill it makes the team coordinated and fully observable — you can open it and read the entire multi-agent exchange."
    },
    {
      "q": "What's Dan's answer to 'this is expensive'?",
      "a": "You're thinking about now, not the future. Models improve, token cost falls, tokens will be available at insane volumes — you (and he) are NOT spending enough tokens. Spend to win; the old single-agent-in-a-terminal way is finished."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is 'trust and scale'?",
      "a": "The 2026 game: raise trust in your agents until you know they'll ship the result on enter, then scale how big and how surgical you can go. Compounding, domain-locked agent experts drive both."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does this only work in Pi? Do you need Claude Code?",
      "a": "It runs on the customizable Pi harness (beyond Claude Code). You could build a more limited version in Claude Code (subagents + hooks). The ideas transfer to any capable tool; Pi adds harness-level control (UI/hooks/tools). Claude Code is the bootloader that installs/deploys it."
    }
  ],
  "confidence_notes": [
