274:              <tr><td>Orchestrator</td><td>Opus 4.6</td><td>Classify, create a TillDone list for substantial work, delegate to teams, synthesize</td><td>Reads broadly; may update its own expertise, but does not execute application work</td></tr>
275:              <tr><td>Team lead</td><td>Opus 4.6</td><td>Understand the team question, delegate to members, combine specialist evidence</td><td>May update its own mental model; no bash; application work belongs to workers</td></tr>
276:              <tr><td>Worker</td><td>Sonnet 4.6</td><td>Execute the assigned specialist task precisely inside its domain</td><td>Has read/write/edit/bash subject to domain enforcement</td></tr>
424:          <p>Bash is harder because a shell string can hide writes. The enforcer uses heuristics to detect write and delete patterns rather than assuming that <code>bash</code> is harmless. This does not turn shell parsing into a perfect sandbox; it does make the course’s domain contract structural across the ordinary file tools and common shell mutations.</p>
831:              <p><strong>Orchestrator + leads · Opus 4.6 in the course.</strong> They interpret ambiguous goals, choose routes, preserve dependencies, reconcile specialist evidence, and compress it into the next decision.</p>
836:              <p><strong>Workers · Sonnet 4.6 in the course.</strong> They receive a narrower assignment, use tools, and execute precisely inside a bounded specialist domain.</p>
850:          <p>The teams propose classifiers, plan a benchmark, engineer the comparison, and validate the result. In the recorded head-to-head, Logistic Regression performs better on LOW, ComplementNB performs better on MID, and both are perfect on HIGH; the synthesis keeps Logistic Regression as the default and names ComplementNB a credible challenger. The footer shows roughly <strong>$8.28</strong> for the full run.</p>
871:          <p>The repository includes <code>specs/fix-worker-cost-tracking.md</code>, a real defect report. In the documented case, the TUI showed $8.79 while the API dashboard showed $34.28—about a quarter of the real spend. Worker costs were being dropped across layers. The fix had to propagate costs from member subprocesses through leads into the parent footer.</p>
1218:          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>The tree shows ten agents: 1 + 3 + 6.</li><li>The Orchestrator/leads are on Opus 4.6 in this repo and workers on Sonnet 4.6.</li><li>Per-agent costs are visible.</li><li>Your durable statement is “stronger coordination tier, capable bounded execution tier,” not a memorized model ID.</li></ul></div>

