vol-3-ma/16-lead-agents.html:413:            <p><strong>Domain locking:</strong> explicit per-agent, per-path permissions that control reading, creating/updating, and deleting, enforced by the runtime rather than trusted to the prompt alone.</p>
vol-3-ma/16-lead-agents.html:427:          <p>Bash is harder because a shell string can hide writes. The enforcer uses heuristics to detect write and delete patterns rather than assuming that <code>bash</code> is harmless. Per-path guarantees apply to the ordinary file tools. The Bash checks are a coarse secondary guard: they notice common mutation patterns and then ask whether the agent has any upsert or delete permission at all; they do not authorize the target path and are not a sandbox. A critical boundary test must therefore use <code>write</code> or <code>edit</code>, not shell redirection.</p>
