62:        <p class="hero__promise"><strong>Mastery promise.</strong> By the end, you will be able to draw the three-tier architecture from memory, explain why a blocked lead is a successful route, distinguish soft instructions from hard domain enforcement, inspect the source of an agent’s model/tools/skills/domain, operate and reconfigure the real ten-agent team, audit its conversation log, and teach why trust must precede scale.</p>
127:          <li><strong>Evidence</strong>Use the footer, session directory, logs, source files, and blocked tool calls to verify claims about behavior.</li>
132:          <p>The prompt-routing classifier shown in the video is a separate codebase and is not shipped here. The lab runs the multi-team tool against its own source repository, exactly as its README’s local test instructs. Live team runs consume paid model tokens; keep prompts small, record costs, and do not launch the scaled preset casually.</p>
136:        <p>Every major idea repeats the same ten moves: hook, picture, frame-backed story, name, worked source example, small practice, fail/fix, current-practice note, retrieval, and teach-back. Do the small practice before revealing the answers. In the lab, collect evidence rather than impressions. “It seemed like a team” is weak. “The lead’s tool call was blocked, a worker ran the read, and the session log records both” is inspectable.</p>
509:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">23:40</span>Three storage layers are visible: the append-only team conversation, persistent per-agent sessions, and session-level work products. Expertise is the curated long-lived layer, not a transcript dump.</figcaption>
530:          <p>After meaningful work, Backend Dev might record the application’s API layer, a key schema decision, a queueing pattern, a scaling observation, and one unresolved question. It should not paste the entire source file, store the full conversation log, or preserve raw test output. Those belong in source, <code>conversation.jsonl</code>, or a session artifact.</p>
538:            <li>Write a five-line YAML mental-model entry with the source path and a concise conclusion.</li>
742:          <p>The shipped repository makes the idea testable with five presets. The default provides three balanced teams. Engineering-only strips the system to one execution team. Planning + Engineering removes Validation. Plan-and-scale keeps Planning and creates three Engineering teams. The open-source configuration demonstrates model overrides across providers.</p>
1043:          <h3>The architecture diagram that never meets the source</h3>
1045:          <p>Dan’s lesson invites source-level inspection. The course repo contains the chat extension, delegation extension, domain enforcer, task extension, configuration loader, prompt renderer, subprocess runner, logs, themes, specs, and agent definitions. The runtime claim can be traced.</p>
1081:          <p>The system prompt is templated with <code>{{SESSION_DIR}}</code>, <code>{{CONVERSATION_LOG}}</code>, team/member blocks, expertise blocks, and skill blocks. The agent definition remains readable source; the loader resolves the current runtime facts into it.</p>
1145:        <p class="section-lead">The chapter is offline-first and complete without video. These two optional companions are the only YouTube overlays used: both come from the lesson’s own resource list and address named gaps rather than repeating the lesson.</p>
1167:        <p class="section-lead">The course repository is the tool. You will run it against its own source, observe the three tiers, inspect the agent contracts, reconfigure the roster, trigger a hard domain block, and prove conversation awareness from the session log. The separate classifier shown in the video is not part of this lab.</p>
1247:          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Checkpoint 4 · audit the anatomy from source</h3>
